            <ArticleTitle>Involvement of transient receptor potential vanilloid subfamily 1 in endothelin-1-induced pain-like behavior.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>233-7</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1097/WNR.0b013e32831befa5</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Although local administration of endothelin-1 (ET-1) is known to evoke spontaneous pain, the mechanism of ET-1-induced pain has not been elucidated. We investigated the involvement of protein kinase C (PKC) and transient receptor potential vanilloid subfamily 1 (TRPV1) in ET-1-induced pain-like behavior. Intraplantar ET-1 evoked pain-like behaviors, including licking, flinching, and biting, in a dose-dependent manner in wild-type mice. ET-1-induced pain-like behavior was attenuated by an endothelin type A receptor antagonist but not by PKC inhibitors and was also attenuated in TRPV1-deficient (KO) mice. In addition, we found a significant reduction of spinal Fos expression caused by the same dose of ET-1 in KO mice compared with that in wild-type mice. This study showed that endothelin type A receptor and TRPV1 are involved in ET-1-induced pain-like behaviors but failed to reveal the contribution of PKC.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
